# Biome 11: La Cumbre. Format: see ../build.py.
# The sheet for biome 10 was being written at the same time as this one: the join below describes this side only.
# Revised 11-13, late, to Parker's third round of answers (ANSWERS.md, items 11, 12 and 13): the summit reads as the ruin of a ring,
# the frogs' hot spring is reached by going west past the belt, and every wall and mass follows the painted rule.

BIOME = {
    "name": "La Cumbre",
    "line": "The summit, 962 to 1,000 m, above the clouds. The game's ending. One dome of black ice, a crown of gold, the ruin of the first ring, a last leap to a bell rope, and the belt on a post. West of the belt, for whoever has climbed the mountain: the frogs' hot spring, and a slide all the way down.",
    "story": [
        "Above the storm there is no weather. The cloud lies below you like a white floor, the sun is low and gold, and the last 38 m of the mountain stand up out of it: one dome of black ice with snow on its back, and, standing clear of the dome on its east side, a ring of five rock teeth and two horns of rock. The dome pours east, off a prow and down past the storm ridge. So every miss up here is the same fall: the dome, the prow, the whole mountain.",
        "This is the First Top Dog's ring. He was the first to climb the Peak, and he would fight nowhere else, so anyone who wanted his belt had to make the climb first. The people who built the temple to him forged the belt in the chimney and carried a ring up here in pieces. They capped the five teeth with gold for a crown. They cut the two horns square to be the ring's corner posts, set a timber head with a red pad on each, strung the ring's three ropes round them, and laid the floor and its canvas between. On the very top, at the ringside, they hung the timekeeper's bell, to start and end a bout. Nobody knows that a challenger ever got this far. The belt has waited on the bell's post ever since.",
        "The weather has had the ring for a long time. It is a ruin, but it is still plainly a ring. Its two corner posts stand 6 m apart with their red pads. On the inner face of each, three ropes are still strung tight, red, cream and jade, and they are still lively enough to throw a luchador. Behind them the back runs of the same three ropes sag from post to post. One beam of the floor still spans between the posts' feet, with the canvas hanging off it as a torn apron. A third post lies fallen on the shelf below. The last of the ones who tried sits in the timekeeper's seat, 4 m under the bell, looking up at it.",
        "One frog lives up here in the open. It keeps a tea shop on the ledge under the prow, and it is the one who hangs the town's paper fresh and lays the marigolds. The rest of the mountain's frogs are on the other side. West of the bell the summit falls away into a notch, and a column of steam stands up out of it. Whoever goes on past the belt and steps off the west end of the bell frame slides down the summit's west face and drops through a steaming hole into a warm pool in a cave, full of frogs. It is the reward for the climb. There are two ways on from it. On the far left a water slide runs down inside the mountain to the long fall line and the Sump: the celebratory drop, all the way down. Or a short climb leads up to a wall of frost, and behind that wall is the tunnel under the dome, which slides you east and puts you out at the tea shop.",
    ],
    "built": [
        "Black ice over rock, the glacier's own: blue-black and glossy, with a hard white glint. The dome, its prow, the tunnel under it, the summit's west face and its eave, the west shoulder and both horns are all of it, and none of it can be held. Snow lies only on the dome's back and on the shoulder, as a white cap set about 1 m back from the line a body slides on, so that line is always a dark wet rim.",
        "Walls and masses are painted, not built of blocks. This is the mountain's rule, and its worked example is the tower top on the wind cliffs. Here it means two materials. Black ice, for the dome, the prow, the shoulder and the two horns: a few big flat planes with a designed outline. Its bands of blue-black in three tones, its white glints, its cracks and its lines of rime are flat painted colour on those planes, as long wavy lines and bands. Tan rock, for the five teeth, the pillars under the horns and the peak's own back: the same, with its bedding painted as long wavy lines in three tones of tan. There is no ink inside a face, nothing painted tapers to a point, and no panel is repeated.",
        "A few big breaks are real shape, and only those get ink: the prow's overhang with its painted shadow, the eave over the frost wall, the three turnbuckle blocks on each post, the step in the fifth tooth and the rib under the perch, each with something on it. No dark slot or recess is cut into any face for show. The tunnel's two mouths and the sink are openings the play needs, and they are the only dark holes on the summit. Outlines lean and are notched, with no long ruled edge. The one exception is a face the ring's builders cut square, and there the cut edge is chipped. Every line a body slides on, every hold, every top you stand on and the inner faces of both horns stay exactly where the course has them. Behind each mass its own body carries on, duller and cooler, as one big form with an uneven skyline.",
        "Little outcrops of tan rock stand out of the teeth, the pillars and the peak's back, a few to each, and they carry the summit's plants: low cushions of moss with small pink flowers, silver star-flowers on short stems, and tufts of gold grass. Each plant is one flat painted card, its parts flush colours on one plane so that only its outline is inked, in the style of the station's foreground plants but made for up here. Nothing grows on ice, and no plant is in the play space.",
        "Summit rock is warm, pale and dry, the colour of old cream. It shows only where you stand: the tea ledge, the shelf under the horns, the west horn's top, the perch under the bell, and the doorstep of the frost wall. Roots stand one layer back. The body of every tooth and the pillar under each horn is a rock piece 1.4 to 1.8 m behind the climb, and only the tip is solid. A sliding body passes in front of them, so nothing on the east side can stop a fall.",
        "The temple's people left gold and bronze. An eight-sided gold boss is pinned to the tip of each tooth, brushed and matte. The bell is bronze, with the First Top Dog's face cast on it. The belt is gold plates on red leather.",
        "The ring is dark squared rock, honey timber, hemp, iron and padded cloth. A corner post is a horn cut square: a shaft of glazed dark rock 2.2 m thick, with three blocks standing out of its inner face like a corner's three padded turnbuckles, each block's face sloping, and on its top a timber head 0.4 m thick with three iron turnbuckle eyes and a red pad. A ring rope is nothing like a rope you climb: it is a band 0.3 m thick, wound in glossy tape, with a fat cushion over the turnbuckle at each end. Each post carries three, strung along the sloping faces of its three blocks: red at the bottom, cream in the middle, jade at the top. So each colour is a matched pair, one rope on each post.",
        "What is left of the rest of the ring stands behind the climb and is weathered grey, so that it can't be taken for a thing to hold. The back runs of the three ropes hang from post to post 2.2 m behind you: slack, faded, matte and thinner than the live ones. The floor's last beam spans between the pillars 2.3 m behind you, with the cream canvas and its faded pug's face hanging off it as an apron. Timber you can reach is honey. Timber you can't is grey.",
        "Rime is the summit's own stuff: hard grey-white frost that the wind builds in feathers on anything that stands in it. It makes a collar under each gold boss, a nose on the tea ledge's end and a block on the shelf. It is pale and matte, cracked across with a corner gone, and it grows toward the west, into the wind. The frost wall at the top of the tunnel is rime too, grown where the spring's steam meets the tunnel's cold.",
        "The bell frame is the biggest made thing: a post 6.6 m tall footed in the very top, a beam 6.8 m long and one brace, in honey timber, with marigolds wound up the post. It is the timekeeper's place, at the ringside.",
        "Paper and marigolds are the town's, whole and bright. Every line of paper is tied on thick old ring rope, 2 m behind the climb, and passes at least 2.5 m above any place you can stand, so no line reads as a way across. The line to the bell is carried on a flagstaff lashed behind the west post's timber head, and a taller staff behind the bell frame's post carries its far end. Garlands swag from tooth to tooth, 2 m back.",
        "The tea shop is the frog's own work: planks, a striped cloth, a tin stove pipe, in a room in the tea ledge's back wall, cut away at the front. It is lined with planks and lit warm to its back, so it never reads as a dark slot in the rock. The spring's cave is the mountain's inside, warm: rounded ochre and cream rock crusted with mineral, with no ice in it, painted in long wavy bands like every other mass. What the frogs keep in it is the tea shop's kind of thing: a low table, cups, folded towels, candle lanterns.",
        "Never here: steel, brick, painted metal, storm cloud, lightning, a cloud or a line of paper in front of the climb, a glowing material, a rope that looks like a ring rope and can be held, or a streak or wedge painted on a wall.",
    ],
    "light": "Gold, from one low sun in the east: the far view's own sun. There is no second sun and no sky card of ours. Everything that faces east is lit warm: the teeth, the pillars, the bell. West sides and undersides are in long blue-violet shadow, so the prow's underside, the tunnel, the summit's west face and the west shoulder are the dark parts of the picture. Gold dust drifts in the air. "
             "Two places have a warm light of their own, shown as amber colour on their walls: the tea shop, from its lantern and its stove, and the frogs' spring, from three candle lanterns and from daylight coming down the sink.",
    "looks": [
        ["You can hold it", "Brushed gold: the five bosses of the crown. Honey timber: the west post's timber head, the bell frame's post, beam and brace, the toll handles. Hemp: the bell rope, and the rope of the frogs' stair. Rust-orange iron: the three rings and the bell's yoke. Bronze: the bell. Warm pale rock, where you stand. Grey-white rime, for 3 seconds. All of it dry and matte, and all of it in the play space."],
        ["You can't", "Black ice: blue-black and glossy, with a hard white glint. The dome, the prow, the tunnel, the summit's west face and its eave, the west shoulder, the horns from foot to tip, and the underside of the shelf. The ring ropes are glossy too: you cannot hold one. In the spring, wet dark rock: the sink, the cave's walls above the steam, the slide."],
        ["It throws you", "A ring rope: a glossy band 0.3 m thick and 4.4 m long, in red, cream or jade, with a fat cushion at each end. It is drawn exactly along the line that throws you, and it stands 0.2 m proud of the black ice behind it (note 98). There are six, three on the inner face of each post, and between them they cover the whole face. At rest each one quivers a little. When you hit one it squashes in, springs back and hums, as the garden's caps do, and it throws you up and across to the other post harder than you came in. The cushions throw you as well: every part of a live rope that is drawn does the same thing. A live rope is tight, straight and glossy, and it lies on a post's face. The ring's back ropes are none of those things, and they hang 2.2 m behind you."],
        ["It leaves", "Rime: a collar, a nose or a block of grey-white frost feathers, pale and matte, cracked across with a corner gone. It holds like rock for 3 seconds. In the last second it ticks, sheds frost dust and its crack opens. Then it breaks away in pieces and falls. Twelve seconds later it is back, and you see it come: feathers grow out from the rock toward the west over the last 4 seconds, and only when it is whole can it be taken. What is drawn is exactly what holds. Its job is to leave you hanging from the gold boss above it, in clear air."],
        ["It rings", "The bell: bronze, 2.2 m across, hanging in an iron yoke under the beam's end. The rope comes out of its mouth from the clapper, 10 m long, with a wooden toll handle on its end. When your weight comes onto the rope the bell tips toward you, the clapper shows and strikes, and the bell rocks back. Every hard swing or tug tolls it again; hang still and it rocks to rest (note 100). The catch at the end of the last leap is the loudest toll. Bell, clapper and rope move as one thing. The bell is solid wherever it is drawn: it can be held and stood on."],
        ["It breaks open", "The frost wall: a wall of hoarfrost about 4.3 m tall across the top of the tunnel under the dome, at the foot of the summit's west face. It has the rime's look: grey-white and matte, cracked across, with a corner gone at its foot, which the tea frog uses as its door. You come to it from the spring's side, by the frogs' stair, and you stand on its doorstep. Take it and it holds for 3 seconds, ticks, and breaks away down the tunnel. Step through, and the tunnel slides you east to the tea shop (note 102). It grows back in plain view, so the door shuts behind you."],
        ["It is warm", "Steam and colour. White steam off the kettle, off the spout in the tea shop's wall, thick over the spring's pool, and in a column that stands up out of the notch west of the bell frame, where you see it from the beam. Warm water is milky blue-green. Warm rock is ochre and cream with no frost on it."],
        ["It blows", "Nothing on the summit pushes you. The air is thin and almost still, and that is how you know you are above the storm. What wind there is comes from the west, as it has since the cliffs, and it is shown in the world: every line of paper lifts toward the east, the apron stirs, snow smokes off the prow's lip, the steam column leans east at its top, and rime grows into it. The long wavy lines (note 89) are kept for a wind that pushes. If an area adds one, it gets the lines across its whole reach and a flag at each edge."],
        ["The way on", "From the shelf under the horns the camera holds the west post's lowest rope on screen with you (note 98), and a frog bounces up the ropes now and then to show the move. The two posts with their red pads, the apron, the back ropes and the fallen post say that this is a ring. From the west post's top a line of paper runs to the bell, over the line of the last leap. From the bell frame a second line of paper runs on west over the notch, the steam stands under it, and a painted board on the frame's back strut shows a frog in a pool."],
        ["It ends the climb", "The belt: gold plates on red leather, 1.2 m wide, the pug's face on its centre plate, on the top of the bell's post. It is the forge's work and nothing else up here looks like it. Touch it and the climb is over (note 101). The mountain does not end there: the way west is past it."],
        ["It is scenery", "Rock one layer back: the bodies of the teeth, the pillars under the horns and the peak's own back, tan and duller than the rock you stand on. The lines of paper and the garlands. The ring's remains: the back ropes, the last beam and its apron, the east post's timber head and the fallen post, all in weathered grey and all 1.5 m or more behind you. The tea shop in its room. The frogs and their things. Then our own low cloud, and then the far view that is given."],
    ],
    "layers": [
        ["Background", "Nearest, 1.4 to 3 m back: the rock pieces (the teeth, the pillars, and the peak's own back, which never stops inside the frame), the ring's last beam with its apron, the back ropes, the garlands, the lines of paper, the tea shop in its room, the steam column in the west. Then our own cloud: three low banks 5, 10 and 22 m behind the climb, with their tops between 957 and 966 m, so the summit stands in cloud to its ankles. They are plain white, in a few big calm shapes, and every one of them stands behind the climb (note 40). Then what is given: a calm sea of painted cloud below and all round you, far peaks standing out of it, and the low sun. The area's old pale sky card and its own sun stay out. Open sky is the biggest thing on screen."],
        ["Play space", "The tea ledge and its rime nose. The two iron rings of the climb. The five gold bosses and their rime collars. The shelf and its rime block. The two posts and their six ring ropes. The west post's top and its timber head. The bell rope, the bell, its yoke, the beam and the post. The perch and the one who sits on it. The belt. On the west side: the summit's west face and its eave, the sink, the pool, the bank, the frogs' stair, the doorstep, the frost wall, the tunnel, the water slide."],
        ["Foreground", "Only the two white snow caps, on the dome and on the west shoulder, each in front of solid ice. Neither may cover the tunnel, the sink or the spring: those are cut away to the camera. Gold dust and a little blown snow pass close to the camera. Never a cloud, a line of paper, a garland or steam in front of the climb."],
    ],
    "kit": [
        ["The summit's body, in black ice: the dome with its prow, the tunnel, the summit's west face, the west shoulder. Each is one painted piece: big flat planes, a designed outline, and its bands, glints, cracks and rime painted as long wavy lines. Snow cap: white, matte, set about 1 m back from the sliding line. Summit rock, warm and pale: the tea ledge, the shelf, the west post's top, the perch, the doorstep", "the dome is 38 m tall; ice planes 4 to 10 m across; two caps of about 35 m by 30 m; rock 17 m, 2.6 m, 2.6 m, 2.1 m and 1.9 m", "ice is every surface that slides. Held: none of it. Real breaks: the prow's lip, the tunnel's two mouths, the eave. The caps are never solid and never over open air. Rock is the five places you stand. Held: all of it, but the shelf's underside is black ice", "Play space and round it; the caps in the foreground, in front of solid ice", "the game draws the ice and the rock today in a repeated square panel, and the caps are flat cards; each mass re-made as one painted piece is new (the walls and masses rule)"],
        ["Tooth of the crown: a rock needle one layer back, and the gold boss on its tip. The needle is one painted piece of tan rock in two or three slanted planes, with wavy bedding lines and a leaning, notched outline", "needle 2.5 to 3.4 m wide at its foot, 6 to 24 m tall, most of it below the frame; boss 0.52 m across, eight-sided", "La Corona: five. Held: the boss only. One real break: the step in the fifth tooth, which the shelf is the front edge of", "Play space (the boss); background, 1.4 m back", "the boss is in the kit as a shape; its model and the painted needle are new"],
        ["Rime: collar, nose, block. Each one whole, breaking, and growing back", "collar 1.6 m by 0.5 m; nose 0.8 m by 0.45 m; block 1 m by 0.8 m", "the steps that leave, confirmed as rime frost: a collar 1.8 m under each of the first four bosses, the nose on the tea ledge's east end, the block on the shelf under the fifth boss. Held: all of it, for 3 seconds", "Play space", "new; the game draws carved stone blocks today"],
        ["Iron ring on an eye bolt, at the tip of a rock spur that stands one layer back", "ring 0.52 m across", "three. One hangs under the prow's lip: the launch for the crown, and the summit's one last chance for a body that comes off the prow slowly. One is 3.7 m west of the west post's timber head and level with its top, at 998.2 m: the launch of El Último Salto, and the anchor of the post's stay (note 99). One carries the rope of the frogs' stair, in the sink. Held: the ring only", "Play space; the spur behind", "in the kit as a shape; the model and the spur are new"],
        ["Corner post of the ring: a horn cut square. A shaft of dark rock glazed with black ice, with three turnbuckle blocks on its inner face, one above the other, each with a sloping face, and an iron band painted round the shaft at each rope's eye. One painted piece each. The pillar under it, in tan rock, with its top 2 m cut as a plinth", "west shaft 2.2 m thick, top at 995.2 m; east shaft 2.2 m thick after the move below, tip at 999.9 m; blocks 2.5 m deep and 3.6 m tall; pillars 26 m tall", "Las Cuerdas del Campeón: two. Held: nothing but the west post's flat top. The east shaft is 0.6 m thick in the course today: its outer face moves 1.6 m east, so the two posts match. Inner faces and the east tip do not move", "Play space; the pillar 1.8 m back", "the game draws them today as saw-toothed blocks in a repeated panel; the painted posts are new"],
        ["Ring rope, with a turnbuckle cushion and an iron eye at each end: red, cream, jade", "4.4 m long, 0.3 m thick; cushions 0.5 m", "Las Cuerdas del Campeón: six, in three matched pairs, each lying exactly along its bouncing line (note 98). Red is the lowest on each post, cream the middle, jade the top. It squashes and springs. Thrown from: its whole length, cushions and all. Held: never", "Play space", "new; nothing is drawn today"],
        ["Back run of a ring rope: faded, matte, frayed", "0.3 m thick; the jade one 12.5 m, the snapped ends 1.2 to 2 m", "the ropes that ran round the back of the ring. The jade one still spans the top, from the flagstaff to the east post's head, and the town's paper hangs on it. The red and the cream ones have snapped: their ends hang down the backs of the two posts. So each colour reads as one rope that went round the ring, and nothing hangs across the gap you fly through. Held: never. Thrown from: never", "Background, 2.2 m back", "new"],
        ["Timber head of a corner post: square timber, three iron turnbuckle eyes, a red pad on top", "0.4 m thick; 3 m each, and a fallen one broken off at 2.2 m", "on the west post's top, in honey: climbed like a turnbuckle and jumped from (note 99), and the backstop of the last rope's throw. Held: all of it. The east post's head, standing on the back of the east shaft's top 1.5 m behind you, and the fallen third post on the shelf are scenery, in weathered grey. The fourth post went over the edge long ago", "Play space (one); background, 1.5 m back (two)", "in the kit: ring_post; the grey ones are a second finish"],
        ["The ring's floor: its last beam, five broken joist ends, the canvas folded over it and hanging as an apron, cream, torn, with a faded pug's face", "beam 5.6 m long, 0.4 m deep; apron 5 m wide, 1.7 m tall", "between the two pillars at 984 m, under the lowest ropes, in the shelf's view and the crown's. Weathered grey timber. It hangs behind the line of every fling", "Background, 2.3 m back", "new; it takes the place of the canvas strip"],
        ["The frog that shows the move (note 98, confirmed)", "0.3 m", "it sits on the fallen post, behind the shelf. The first time your feet are on the shelf, and then about every 25 seconds while you stand on the shelf or its block or hang from the fifth boss, it jumps into the west post's lowest rope and is thrown from post to post up all six, to the west post's top. It hops off the back of the top and is next seen on the fallen post again. It never starts while you are in the air, it is never solid, and it is always ahead of you", "Play space, in passing; background at rest", "new, the one frog model"],
        ["Bell frame: post, beam, brace", "post 0.5 m thick, 6.6 m tall; beam 6.8 m; brace 4 m", "on the very top: the timekeeper's place. Held: all of it. Top Dog is climbed along the beam to the post's top. The beam's west end, 1 m past the post, is where you step off for the spring", "Play space", "in the kit: bell_frame, to be re-made at the course's size"],
        ["The bell, its clapper, its iron yoke; the bell rope with a wooden toll handle", "bell 2.2 m across, 2.6 m tall; yoke 3.1 m; rope 10 m; handle 0.5 m by 0.3 m", "La Campana, under the beam's east end. The rope is the catch of the last leap and the climb to the bell, and the bell swings and tolls with it (note 100). Held: the rope, the handle, the yoke, and the bell wherever it is drawn. The bell is solid, confirmed: it can be held and stood on", "Play space", "in the kit: summit_bell, and the game's rope; the clapper, the swing and the solid bell are new"],
        ["The last of the ones who tried, sitting; his marigolds", "1.2 m tall; pile 0.8 m", "on the perch in the summit's east cliff, 4 m under the bell, looking up at it: the timekeeper's seat. He is the game's climber model, posed sitting, and nothing is modelled for him: the new climber model takes his place when it comes", "Play space; the marigolds behind", "the game poses its climber there today; in the kit: marigold_pile"],
        ["The belt on the post; the belt held", "1.2 m by 0.6 m", "Top Dog. On the post's top it ends the climb. In the ceremony it is a thing the climber holds: a hand on each strap end, the plates to the camera, lifted over the head (note 101). The climber in the ceremony is the game's climber model, posed and turned: nothing is modelled for it, and it waits for the new climber model", "Play space", "in the kit: champion_belt; the held one is new, a prop only"],
        ["Ceremony rays", "each 12 to 30 m long, 1 to 3 m wide at its far end", "the ending: about eight flat cut-out blades in cream, yellow and the town's paper colours, fanned out from the climber and wheeling round as he turns. Bright flat colour against the sky, no glow. They stand behind him", "Background, 1.5 m back", "new, an effect (note 101)"],
        ["The ceremonious orchestra", "an effect, no model", "the ending: the mountain's four-note call, resolved at last, for a full orchestra with the summit's mariachi and the bell in it, in place of today's short fanfare", "All", "new, music (note 101)"],
        ["The rest of the ending: three tolls, the town's fireworks, confetti, the TOP DOG card, the flag", "fireworks as small cut-out bursts; the card as the kit's", "after the ceremony. The fireworks come up through the cloud sea in the east, far below and small", "Background, far; the card in front of everything", "in the kit: peak_summit_card, flag_top_dog, the house confetti; the fireworks are the far town's"],
        ["Line of paper, whole and bright, on thick old ring rope; flagstaff. Marigold garland", "paper 9 m, 12.5 m, 23 m and 30 m; staff 6.3 m; garlands 7 to 12 m long, 0.5 m thick", "paper: across the tea ledge's mouth as the shop's awning; post to post; from the staff behind the west post's head to the bell staff; from the bell staff on west over the notch. All above any hand. Garlands: swagged from tooth to tooth round the crown; wound up the bell's post and along its beam. The tea frog hangs the paper and lays the marigolds", "Background, 2 m back; the frame's garlands flat against its timber", "in the kit: papel_string, marigold_garland; the staff is new"],
        ["The tea shop: a plank counter with a striped cloth, a small iron stove with a tin pipe, the kettle, pot and cups of the cliffs' tea cave, two stools, a shelf of tins, a painted cup for a sign, a lit lantern, and a warm spout in the wall over a stone basin", "room 6 m wide, 2.6 m tall, 2.5 m deep; counter 2.4 m", "in the back wall of the tea ledge, under the prow (note 97). One cup is set out on the counter for you. Steam from the kettle; the pipe's smoke goes up under the prow and out past the lip. The spout is the spring's water, come down through the mountain from the pool", "Background, cut away at the front", "new; the pot, cups and kettle are the tea cave's; the lantern is in the kit: lantern_hang, lit, in place of today's cold_lantern"],
        ["The frog, in an apron", "0.3 m", "behind the tea shop's counter, on an upturned crate so it can see over. Now and then it pours a cup", "Background", "new (notes 60 and 97)"],
        ["The eave: a lip of black ice at the foot of the summit's west face", "the last 4.4 m of the face, turned out 3.1 m to the west", "over the frost wall and its doorstep. It turns a sliding body off its lip over the sink, clear of the doorstep. Held: none of it", "Play space", "new, a change to the dome's west corner (note 102)"],
        ["The sink: a round steaming hole in the floor of the notch, and the shaft under it, in smooth wet warm rock", "mouth 4.5 m wide; shaft 3 m across, 4.3 m deep on its east side and 8.7 m on its west", "at the foot of the summit's west face, where the shoulder pours into the tunnel today. Whatever comes down the west side falls in, and drops through the cave's roof into the deep end of the pool. Held: nothing but the stair's rope and ring, on its east side", "Play space, cut away at the front", "new (note 102)"],
        ["The frogs' hot spring: a cave of ochre and cream rock, a pool of milky blue-green water, a dry bank, rounded rim stones, thick white steam, three candle lanterns, a low table with a pot and cups, folded towels", "cave 15.6 m wide, 3 to 4 m tall; pool 8.8 m wide, 0.8 m deep, its surface 3.4 m under the cave's roof; bank 4.2 m", "inside the west shoulder, under the notch. Nothing in it can hurt you. Held: the bank and the rim stones. The walls above the steam are dark and wet", "Play space; back wall 3 m behind; cut away at the front", "new; the water is the game's; lanterns in the kit: lantern_hang; pot and cups are the tea cave's"],
        ["The frogs of the spring", "0.3 m each; about thirty", "in the water to their eyes, along the back rim, on a ledge of the back wall, on the bank. All the same little frog as every biome's. A few have brought what their biome's frog has: a teacup, a blanket, a ticket. Now and then one goes down the slide on a tea tray", "Background, 1.3 m or more behind you", "new, the one frog model"],
        ["The frogs' stair: a hemp rope with a wooden toll handle, hung from an iron ring; the doorstep", "rope 4.5 m; doorstep 1.9 m wide", "up the east side of the sink, from the bank to the frost wall: 7.3 m. Take the handle from the bank, climb the rope to its ring, and pull up onto the doorstep under the eave. The rope is the bell rope's kind and the ring is the summit's. Held: all of it", "Play space", "the game's rope; rings in the kit as a shape; the doorstep is summit rock"],
        ["Frost wall: hoarfrost across the tunnel's top, cracked, a corner gone at its foot. Whole, breaking, and growing back", "0.9 m thick, about 4.3 m tall", "the door between the spring and the tunnel, under the eave, at the place of the arrow in note 102. Held: all of it, for 3 seconds. Then it breaks away down the tunnel, and it is back 12 seconds later", "Play space", "new"],
        ["The teahouse chute: the tunnel under the dome, as it is. A woven mat on the tea ledge where it puts you out", "21 m long, 3.6 m across; mat 2 m by 1 m", "from behind the frost wall, east and down under the dome, to the west end of the tea ledge, 1 m above its floor. Held: nothing. It is black ice, one painted piece with the dome", "Play space; the mat drawn on the floor", "the tunnel is there today; the mat is new"],
        ["Water slide: a smooth wet tube with warm water running down it; its mouth, wreathed in marigolds", "2 m across; the mouth is 2.2 m wide, behind a rim 0.3 m above the pool", "the celebratory drop, on the spring's far left (note 102). It carries you down inside the mountain to the main fall line, the one that ends in the Sump. It leaves this biome through its floor, at 962 m. Proposed: it comes out as a steaming spout over the gap at the top of the glacier, at about 886 m. That end is for the glacier's and the storm ridge's designs. Held: nothing", "Play space", "new"],
        ["The frog's sign for the spring: a painted board, a frog in a pool under a curl of steam, no words", "0.7 m square", "on the bell frame's back strut, under the beam's west end. A second one, a frog on a slide, is on the cave's back wall over the slide's mouth", "Background, 1.5 m back", "new; the tea shop's sign is its fellow"],
        ["Behind everything. The peak's own back: warm tan rock, one painted piece in big slanted planes with wavy bedding lines and an uneven skyline. Our own cloud: three low banks, plain white, in a few big calm shapes", "rock 74 m wide, 40 m tall; cloud 130 m wide, tops at 957 to 966 m", "the rock 3 m behind the climb, so the mountain never stops inside the frame; the cloud 5, 10 and 22 m behind it, under the whole summit", "Background", "flat cards today; the painted rock, and the cloud re-made calm to match the far sea, are new"],
        ["The summit's plants, on little outcrops: a cushion of moss with small pink flowers, a silver star-flower, a tuft of gold grass", "0.3 to 0.6 m; flat painted cards", "on the outcrops of the teeth, the pillars and the peak's back, a few to each, all 1.4 m or more behind you. Never on ice. Held: never", "Background", "new, made for the summit in the style of the station's foreground plants"],
        ["Gold dust; blown snow off the prow's lip; steam", "effects, no models", "dust over the whole summit; snow at the lip; steam at the shop, over the pool, and in a column up out of the notch, behind the climb", "All", "dust is in the game today; the others are new"],
    ],
    "arrive": "From the storm ridge's headwall, up the last broken rungs of its iron ladder, onto the east end of a rock ledge at 964 m. This side of the join is ours. The ledge is 17 m long, and the prow of the summit's dome roofs it, 4 to 5 m overhead. You come up out of grey cloud into gold light, with the cloud sea at your feet to the east. The first things you see are a line of whole bright paper across the ledge's mouth, a thread of steam, and the tea shop's lantern in the back wall. "
              "Two things keep the ledge from being quite safe: the dome's whole fall line pouring off the lip over your head, and the black mouth of the tunnel at its west end, with a cold draught coming out of it. That tunnel is the way home from the frogs' spring: one day you come out of it yourself. The storm ridge's side of the join is for its own sheet.",
    "leave": "There is no biome above. You leave by the ending: challenge 11.6, Top Dog, and note 101. From the bell you climb onto the beam and along it to the post's top, and you touch the belt. Then a ceremony plays on you. The belt comes off the post into your hands and you lift it over your head with both arms. You turn slowly on the spot and rise off the post into the air above the summit. Long flat rays fan out from you and wheel as you turn. The orchestra plays the mountain's call, resolved, and the bell tolls three times. "
             "The town's fireworks come up through the cloud far below. Then the TOP DOG card with your time and your falls, the confetti, and the wait for a button, as today. From then on the Peak behind the title screen flies your flag. How long the ceremony runs, and whether a button skips it, are settled when it is built. "
             "As this sheet has it, the game then goes on. The button lifts the card and sets you down on the post's top, with your time kept and the clock stopped. West of you the steam stands up out of the notch. Step off the west end of the beam, and the mountain's west side takes you down to the frogs' hot spring. From there you choose: the water slide on the far left, all the way down to the Sump, or the frost wall and the tunnel back to the tea shop. Whether the game goes on after the card is the one open question, on the ceremony's page.",
    "motifs": [
        "The ring: what the whole climb was for. The temple tested you, the forge made the belt, and this is the ring the belt was fought for in: two corner posts, three ropes, an apron, a timekeeper's bell. The belt was made in the chimney's forge, 500 m below, and it waits on the bell's post.",
        "The pug: cast on the bell, on the belt's centre plate, faded on the apron.",
        "The ones who tried: one, the last. He sits in the timekeeper's seat, 4 m under the bell, looking up, with marigolds laid beside him. There is nobody above him.",
        "The town follows you up: its paper is whole and bright here for the first time since the temple, and its marigolds are on the crown and the bell. When you take the belt its fireworks come up through the cloud. The town itself is under the cloud and not seen from here: the fireworks are how you know it saw you.",
        "The fall: the dome under every move, pouring east. The last leap is the worst fall in the game, with the bell in view. The mercy is one iron ring under the prow's lip. And once the belt is yours the same long fall is given back as a gift: the slide from the spring, all the way down.",
        "Tea: the mountain's second tea place, and one thread with the tea cave on the wind cliffs. It is the same pot, the same cups and the same kettle. There it was a low table and two cups in a cave; here it is a counter, a row of cups and a sign. A cup is set out for you both times, and a third time on the bank of the spring.",
        "The frog: this biome's frog keeps the tea shop, in an apron. And all the frogs are here: the hot spring west of the belt is full of them, and it is theirs. The tea frog goes to work through the corner of the frost wall and down the tunnel.",
    ],
    "notes": [
        "Note 97: the ledge at 964 m becomes a tea shop kept by a frog. Parker: \"make this area a little tea shop with a frog\".",
        "Note 98: from the shelf under the horns he could not tell where to go, because the six ring ropes are not drawn at all. Parker: \"where do we go from here?\" and then \"oh there is invisible bouncy stuff\". Each rope gets a model lying exactly along its bouncing line, with padded turnbuckles; the camera at the shelf shows the lowest rope; a frog shows the move. Our call from it: every gold boss, rime step, rope, post, ring and bell has a kit line that says what is held and what it looks like.",
        "Parker, 11-13, late, on the summit's story: \"Yes this is all correct but the summit currently does not look like the ruin of a ring, it can be reworked a but to look like one\". So the horns are re-made as the ring's two corner posts: squared shafts with three turnbuckle blocks each, timber heads with red pads, three matched pairs of ropes, the back runs of the same ropes from post to post, the floor's last beam with its apron, the fallen third post, and the bell as the timekeeper's. One thing moves in the play space: the east horn's outer face goes 1.6 m east, so its shaft is as thick as the west's. No hold, bouncing line or launch moves.",
        "Confirmed by Parker, 11-13, late: the breaking steps are rime frost; a frog shows the rope move, now and then; the bell is solid. Also taken as settled: the ropes are red, cream and jade; the tea frog hangs the paper and lays the marigolds; no wind on the tea shop's doorstep; a tall staff behind the bell frame carries the paper.",
        "Note 99: the breaking plank goes, and the iron ring moves up level with the top of the corner post, no closer to it. Parker: \"get rid of this breaking platform, and move the piton slightly higher up so it is level with the top of the wood peg, but not closer to it\". You climb the post's timber head like a turnbuckle and jump to the ring.",
        "Note 100: the rope rings the bell, and the bell and its clapper swing with it. Parker: \"make it so you can actually ring the bell when you are dangling on the rope\" and \"like the dangle part needs to move not be static\".",
        "Note 101: the ending is a ceremony. Parker: \"when you get the belt, make have you hold the belt above your head and rotate around and fly into the air with fanning lights coming off you spinning with a ceremonious orchestra\".",
        "Parker, 11-13, late: \"we are getting a new climber model very soon\". So the last of the ones who tried and the climber in the ceremony are the game's climber model, posed. Nothing is modelled for them now.",
        "Note 102, as Parker corrected it, 11-13, late. The first version of this sheet had the frost wall in the tunnel, opening onto the spring. That was the wrong way round. Parker: \"the only way to get over there is to get past the belt, the breakable wall is supposed to lead back to where the teahouse is planned, to the right. To the left, the new area, is where going left off the summit by the belt sends you and is the little cozy reward for climbing the summit, along with the celebratory drop all the way down on the slide\". And of the way out: \"you can slide or climb to the breakable wall that leads to the teahouse chute\".",
        "So, from note 102: you reach the spring by going west off the bell frame, past the belt. The summit's west face slides you into the sink, and the sink drops you into the pool. From the spring there are two ways on: the water slide on the far left, or the frogs' stair up to the frost wall, which is broken from the spring's side and opens the tunnel to the tea shop. Our calls, on the spring's page: the wall breaks 3 seconds after a hand takes it, like all the summit's rime; the slide joins the fall line below this biome; the game goes on after the TOP DOG card.",
        "Parker, 11-13, on walls: \"I like the painted look\", and of the worked example: \"it does not need these random triangles\" and \"just the painted lines look lovely\". The dome, the horns, the teeth and the peak's back follow it: big planes, designed outlines, long wavy lines and bands of flat colour, a few big real breaks, and nothing that tapers to a point.",
        "Note 89: wind is drawn loudly. Nothing on the summit pushes you, so here the wind is shown by paper, the apron, blown snow, steam and rime. The wavy lines are kept for a wind that pushes.",
        "Note 60: a frog in every biome. This biome's is the tea shop's. The spring holds about thirty more, and one more shows the rope move. Note 40: the summit's own near cloud stands behind the climb, and no cloud stands in front of it.",
        "Parker, 11-13, on the sea of cloud as painted banks: \"Too busy\". It is being re-made calmer, and our own three banks are plain white in a few big shapes to match. On the area's old stand-in far cards: \"This will be addressed once we build that area as it is that area's background\". This sheet's answer: the pale sky card and the area's own sun stay out. Rock one layer back and three low banks of our own are all that stand in front of the far view.",
    ],
}
